12. Lesson 7 — Interview questions and answers
These are important questions for GenAI Engineer interviews.

Q1. How does an LLM generate a response?

An LLM predicts the next token based on the input and the context. It calculates probabilities for possible tokens and uses a decoding strategy to select the next token. This process repeats until the response is complete or a generation limit is reached.

Q2. What is greedy decoding?

Greedy decoding selects the highest-probability token at every generation step. It is simple and predictable but can sometimes produce repetitive responses.

Q3. What is temperature?

Temperature controls the randomness of token selection. Lower temperature produces more predictable responses, while higher temperature allows more variation.

Q4. What is Top-K sampling?

Top-K sampling restricts the candidate tokens to the K highest-probability tokens. The model then selects from those candidates.

Q5. What is Top-P sampling?

Top-P sampling selects a group of high-probability tokens whose cumulative probability reaches a specified threshold. Unlike Top-K, the number of selected tokens can change.

Q6. What is the difference between Top-K and Top-P?

Top-K uses a fixed number of candidate tokens, whereas Top-P uses a cumulative probability threshold to determine the candidate group.

Q7. Which sampling strategy would you use in a banking chatbot?

I would start with a relatively low temperature to encourage consistent answers. I would also use relevant document retrieval, grounding instructions, and output validation to improve reliability.

Q8. Does low temperature eliminate hallucinations?

No. Temperature controls randomness, not factual correctness. I would use RAG, source validation, and appropriate evaluation to reduce hallucinations.

Q9. What are logits?

Logits are raw scores generated by the model for possible next tokens. They are commonly converted into probabilities using Softmax.

Q10. What is the difference between a token and a word?

A word is a language unit, whereas a token is a unit processed by the model. A token may represent a complete word, part of a word, or punctuation.

