Q1. What is a context window?

A context window is the maximum amount of token information a model can process for a request. It includes the supplied instructions, user input, conversation history, retrieved context, and potentially generated output within the applicable limits.

Q2. What happens when the context window is exceeded?

The API request may fail because it exceeds the model's context limit. I would reduce unnecessary conversation history, retrieve only relevant document chunks, summarize older messages, or select a model with a suitable context capacity.

Q3. What is tokenization?

Tokenization is the process of converting text into smaller units called tokens. These tokens are converted into numerical IDs that the model processes.

Q4. What is the difference between input and output tokens?

Input tokens are the information sent to the model, such as prompts, questions, and retrieved documents. Output tokens are generated by the model as its response.

Q5. How do you count tokens in Python?

For supported OpenAI tokenizers, I can use the tiktoken library to encode text and calculate the number of tokens. For other model providers, I would use their supported tokenizer or token-counting functionality.

Q6. How do you manage long conversations in a chatbot?

I would maintain recent conversation history, summarize older messages, and retrieve relevant information when required. I would also monitor token usage to avoid exceeding the context window.

Q7. What is the Lost in the Middle problem?

It refers to the tendency of some models to use information placed at the beginning or end of long contexts more effectively than information buried in the middle. I would improve this by retrieving relevant chunks and organizing the context carefully.

Q8. What is the difference between context window and memory?

The context window is the information the model can process in a particular request. Memory is information retained by an application or platform and supplied again when relevant.

Q9. Why shouldn't we send all documents to an LLM?

Sending all documents increases token usage, cost, and latency. It can also introduce irrelevant information. I would use RAG to retrieve only the relevant document chunks.

Q10. How would you optimize context in a banking RAG application?

I would retrieve relevant chunks from the vector database, use metadata filtering, remove duplicate or irrelevant content, maintain recent conversation history, and summarize older messages.
I would also monitor token usage and evaluate the relevance of the retrieved information.

